$\normii{\nabla f(\Xk)}\geq {\ubar{\varepsilon}}' \mbox{ for all } k\in\N.$

Let $\accolade{\xk}, \accolade{\dk}$, and ${{\varepsilon}}'$ be realizations of $\accolade{\Xk}, \accolade{\Dk}$, and ${\ubar{\varepsilon}}'$, respectively, for which $\normii{\nabla f(\xk)}\geq {{\varepsilon}}'$ for all $k$. Since $\dk\to 0$, there exists $k_0\in\N$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{bChoice}
\dk<b:=\alpha_Q\cdot\min\accolade{\frac{{{\varepsilon}}'}{2(\kappa_h+\kappa_{eg})}, \frac{{{\varepsilon}}'}{2(\eta_2+\kappa_{eg})}, \frac{{{\varepsilon}}'}{\frac{16\kappa_{ef}}{\kappa_{fcd}(1-\eta_1)}+2\kappa_{eg}}, \frac{\dmaxx}{\gamma}}
\end{equation}
for all $k\geq k_0$.
Let $\ubar{r}_k$ be the random variable with realizations $r_k=\log_{\gamma}\left(\frac{\dk}{b}\right)$. Then $r_k<0$ for all $k\geq k_0$. The main idea is to show that such realizations occur only with probability zero, leading to a contradiction. 

To prove that $\accolade{\ubar{r}_k}$ is a submartingale, recall the events $I^Q_k$ and $J^Q_k$ satisfying~\eqref{otherProbIneqs} and $\mathcal{A}_k$ satisfying~\eqref{Akineq}.
